<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Kahlil Gibran</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1883">1883</a>–<a href="/years/1931">1931</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Jubrān Khalīl Jubrān, Gibran</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Kahlil Gibran was a Lebanese-American writer, poet and visual artist, born in Bsharri in the Ottoman-ruled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mount-lebanon">Mount Lebanon</a> Mutasarrifate to a Maronite Christian family. He emigrated with his mother and siblings to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boston">Boston</a> in 1895, studied art in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> from 1908 to 1910 with the help of Mary Haskell, and settled in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-york">New York</a> in 1911. Some of his writings were banned by the Ottoman authorities for their anti-clericalism and calls for rebellion. His book The Prophet, first published in 1923, has been translated into more than 100 languages. He re-founded the Pen League with fellow Mahjari poets in 1920 and died at 48; his body was taken to Bsharri.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/kahlil-gibran.jpg" alt="Kahlil Gibran" width="274" height="379" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Kahlil_Gibran_1913.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Kahlil Gibran was born on 6 January 1883 in Bsharri, in the Mount Lebanon Mutasarrifate. His parents, Khalil Sa'ad Gibran and Kamila Rahmeh, the daughter of a priest, belonged to a Maronite Christian family; Khalil was Kamila's third husband. Gibran had two younger sisters, Marianna and Sultana, and an older half-brother, Boutros, and the family lived in poverty.</p><p>After the family reached <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boston">Boston</a> in 1895, a teacher noticed his creative abilities and introduced him to the photographer and publisher F. Holland Day. At fifteen he was sent back to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lebanon">Lebanon</a> to enrol at the Collège de la Sagesse in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beirut">Beirut</a>, and he returned to Boston in 1902 on the death of his youngest sister. His half-brother and his mother died the following year. His drawings were first shown at Day's studio in January 1904, where he met Mary Haskell.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Works and ideas</h2><p>Most of Gibran's early writings were in Arabic, but most of his work after 1918 was in English. The Madman, his first book published by Alfred A. Knopf, appeared in 1918, and The Forerunner in 1920. Sand and Foam followed in 1926 and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jesus">Jesus</a>, the Son of Man in 1928, a portrait of Jesus through the words of seventy-seven contemporaries that was inspired by his meeting with ʻAbdu'l-Bahá, the leader of the Baháʼí Faith, around 1911 to 1912. The last book published in his lifetime was The Earth Gods, on 14 March 1931.</p><p>Gibran also made more than seven hundred visual artworks, and was confirmed in his aim of becoming a Symbolist painter in a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> studio. Raised a Maronite, he was influenced by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a>, especially <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sufism">Sufi</a> mysticism, and his knowledge of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lebanon">Lebanon</a>'s factional struggles strengthened his belief in the unity of religions.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Gibran was admitted to St. Vincent's Hospital in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manhattan">Manhattan</a> on 10 April 1931 and died there the same day, aged 48, after refusing the last rites. The reported cause was cirrhosis of the liver with incipient tuberculosis in one lung. He had wished to be buried in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lebanon">Lebanon</a>, and his body travelled from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boston">Boston</a> to Providence and then by the liner Sinaia, reaching Bsharri in August. A cousin bought the Mar Sarkis Monastery, which is now the Gibran Museum, and he left all future American royalties on his books to Bsharri for &quot;civic betterment&quot;.</p><p>The popularity of The Prophet grew markedly in the 1960s among the American counterculture and later the New Age movements, and it has never been out of print. A crater on Mercury was named after him in 2009.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Kahlil Gibran” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Writers who emigrated to the Americas, among them Ameen Rihani and <b>Kahlil Gibran</b>, carried its spirit abroad, and the Pen League that Gibran and Mikhail Naimy led in New York from 1920 renewed Arabic poetry.</blockquote><a href="/movement/nahda">Nahda · Legacy</a></li></ul></section>
